    <div class="rec-card priority-1">
      <div class="rec-priority">Priority 1 &bull; Immediate Action</div>
      <h3>Urgently Transition High-Risk Roles</h3>
      <p>Business Analysts (avg risk 0.75) and Data Analysts (0.65) represent the most exposed workforce segments. With median salaries 37% below automation-resilient peers and survival scores averaging 0.43–0.56, these roles face structural displacement by 2030. Begin managed transition programs within 12 months.</p>
      <div class="data-link">Data basis: Business Analyst avg risk 0.75, survival score 0.43; Data Analyst avg risk 0.65, survival score 0.56; High-Risk median salary $21,683 vs. Low-Risk $34,105</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card priority-1">
      <div class="rec-priority">Priority 2 &bull; Immediate Action</div>
      <h3>Invest in Python &amp; Deep Learning Reskilling</h3>
      <p>Python (demand 82.9 in 2035, highest gain) and Deep Learning (highest survival class 1.89) define the safety corridor against automation. AI Researchers and ML Engineers — both Python/Deep Learning roles — hold risk scores below 0.25. Structured reskilling from Excel/SQL toward Python/ML should be the primary workforce investment.</p>
      <div class="data-link">Data basis: AI Researcher avg risk 0.15, ML Engineer 0.25; Python demand growth 79.9→82.9; Deep Learning avg survival class 1.89</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card priority-2">
      <div class="rec-priority">Priority 3 &bull; Near-Term (12–24 months)</div>
      <h3>Prioritize Senior-Level Talent Retention</h3>
      <p>Senior roles command 2.1× the median salary of Entry-level positions ($48,318 vs. $22,924). Given that seniority substantially improves compensation without meaningfully changing automation risk, organizations should focus retention incentives on senior-level staff in low-risk disciplines — particularly Senior AI Researchers, ML Engineers, and Cybersecurity Analysts.</p>
      <div class="data-link">Data basis: Median salary Entry $22,924, Mid $32,996, Senior $48,318; experience level does not change avg risk score (&lt;1% variance)</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card priority-2">
      <div class="rec-priority">Priority 4 &bull; Near-Term (12–24 months)</div>
      <h3>Diversify Geographic Talent Strategy</h3>
      <p>India offers a 3.5× salary cost advantage over the USA ($12,448 vs. $43,422) but carries the lowest survival rate (78.6%) and highest structural risk concentration. A balanced strategy should leverage India for high-volume, transitional roles while locating AI research and cybersecurity functions in USA, UK, or Canada where survival rates exceed 90%.</p>
      <div class="data-link">Data basis: Median salary USA $43,422 vs. India $12,448; survival rate USA 93.8% vs. India 78.6%; Canada 91.0%, UK 90.3%</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card priority-3">
      <div class="rec-priority">Priority 5 &bull; Strategic (2–4 years)</div>
      <h3>Build Cybersecurity &amp; Cloud Bench Strength</h3>
      <p>Cybersecurity Analysts (risk 0.25, survival 1.53) and Cloud Engineers (risk 0.35, survival 1.48) represent the second tier of automation-resilient roles. With Security and AWS skill demand both above 78 through 2035 and stable job openings (~14–16M/yr), these disciplines offer reliable long-term career pathways. Building internal bench depth in these areas hedges against talent market tightening.</p>
